            <ArticleTitle>Hostility, CHD incidence, and total mortality: a 25-year follow-up study of 255 physicians.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>59-63</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>High levels of hostility as assessed by a MMPI scale (Ho) have been found associated with increased levels of arteriographically documented coronary atherosclerosis. In this study we examined the relationship between hostility and subsequent health status in a 25-year follow-up of 255 medical students who completed the MMPI while in medical school. High Ho scores were found to be predictive of both clinical coronary disease incidence and total mortality.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
